{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-112460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-(2)(-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7066aa10d12f19078b3a27ba56611d3a38e8139bc4054fd44694510132822df"} {"question" "q-112460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 4 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(6)(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -10)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(-4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(10)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7066aa10d12f19078b3a27ba56611d3a38e8139bc4054fd44694510132822df"} {"question" "q-109338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b