$A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7066aa10d12f19078b3a27ba56611d3a38e8139bc4054fd44694510132822df"} {"question" "q-112438" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112438" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112438" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 1 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(9)=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7066aa10d12f19078b3a27ba56611d3a38e8139bc4054fd44694510132822df"} {"question" "q-112478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-12-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-12-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-7)\\cdot 7 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda +5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-5)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7066aa10d12f19078b3a27ba56611d3a38e8139bc4054fd44694510132822df"} {"question" "q-112477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-112477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7066aa10d12f19078b3a27ba56611d3a38e8139bc4054fd44694510132822df"} {"question" "q-109095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7066aa10d12f19078b3a27ba56611d3a38e8139bc4054fd44694510132822df"} {"question" "q-109102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"