a -5)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7066aa10d12f19078b3a27ba56611d3a38e8139bc4054fd44694510132822df"} {"question" "q-109315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "q-109315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/state.json" "file_sha256" "c67052bf2f9b4a45f5bf80ae1d28860516e47afe9e7139dda5b8b5ffecbd52e8"} {"question" "e-9087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/1a6c3918b93419f9576951374ebd69b2df271106345493084ffa0797b0a35847.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/example-9087.json" "file_sha256" "c18c3287a9e757afa345c013ceed448e9fa4ac39b221487c76495b65156022ad"} {"question" "e-9087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-4\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-3$ and $\\lambda_2=6.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/d6c87739f52f450b27c68a118bba04e51e68a377da7d0a16ae0658e1b2d547f0.png)\n\n**Note**: A Jordan canonical form is unique up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. Therefore, another Jordan canonical form for this matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/7bd509d943bf0707e75c9436aee3a511f8366502bbf8121b715cec8ce366953b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/example-9087.json" "file_sha256" "c18c3287a9e757afa345c013ceed448e9fa4ac39b221487c76495b65156022ad"} {"question" "e-9226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/4b66afe6c1771afad4987215e3ddc02865a9f873ae93050f940c36bc3ff86356.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/example-9226.json" "file_sha256" "bdfb3d953bfd5fa2f65d5ba2af65ed0dbdd09fe6d7aa2434d767ccd1b7589967"} {"question" "e-9226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda = -3,$ and\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/2be33154f6a1933a12a3c43474464edb9c4e3fd2b2a4213d789b49fc1e4aec77.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/example-9226.json" "file_sha256" "bdfb3d953bfd5fa2f65d5ba2af65ed0dbdd09fe6d7aa2434d767ccd1b7589967"} {"question" "e-9225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/69fcceae9cb181cf79e1b12f0db37ed2ba27841aaebb089ffa24d0d365b9db56.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/example-9225.json" "file_sha256" "40c1870ae550c7a6da0b721946db5cc874951b8fd1b43d578becf83103cc899e"} {"question" "e-9225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/c8716d15efb054f488535b139e201dda7bc61a3fb8880caf50d65bee515c2cc1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/example-9225.json" "file_sha256" "40c1870ae550c7a6da0b721946db5cc874951b8fd1b43d578becf83103cc899e"} {"question" "q-112453" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "b9ad9a953d108eb7e943ed11b1de858008270c68181bda86c768ccd910406175" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived from the authentic matrix A=[[5,-2],[1,8]]: det(A-lambda I)=(5-lambda)(8-lambda)+2=lambda^2-13lambda+42=(lambda-7)(lambda-6). Distinct eigenvalues imply diagonalizability, so diag(7,6) is a Jordan canonical form, original option a (index 0). The worked solution explicitly derives this result and saved grading reports Correct. The stored [[7,1],[0,7]] has trace 14 and determinant 49, whereas A has trace 13 and determinant 42, so it cannot be similar to A. This is a derived source-backed correction, not a direct MA answer key." "bindings" {"problem" "50bb1cfc1407d21d782fcf3030a6be09a4de167607ddce78fa1dafa601ac9223" "worked_solution" "81d19a64dd24f9281a2103a6a0948dc72e0715a0d6cbde6a70d10fc3168f1dd6" "original_field" "23f53742b2bd5b04d9a851d01f423e0256d1df4109f430e07cd678763df1dd56" "reviewed_field" "465b4de7c6777ebdaa95a85c6acd234e4889b1c49e09aee7eeed24beb4430b76" "raw_widget" "66101216d66b6e5cb2533f9f887b36a9c766fa228c0130819e4929a419a3ece3"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-112453-before.png" "sha256" "9a947fbdf192d77e202c8a7300bd210bbef28021aa47856f4a42d1cf540bf584"} {"path" "q-112453-before.json" "sha256" "a358c9b3898f9cf8a904f0ab307641b14dd3fc3e7e148c159e92c3d0701ad184"} {"path" "history-q-112453.png" "sha256" "be2e67aa275943724911b4b65b1a04ae34c95decf2c5458c4a9dc6f17442f2b3"} {"path" "q-112453-after.json" "sha256" "e8bedb5ded25cdf3d10acb12b12282bc7dbc28385f2ab04df1c091e7b68a70d5"}]} {"question" "q-112453" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "b9ad9a953d108eb7e943ed11b1de858008270c68181bda86c768ccd910406175" "rationale" "Derived from the authentic matrix A=[[5,-2],[1,8]]: det(A-lambda I)=(5-lambda)(8-lambda)+2=lambda^2-13lambda+42=(lambda-7)(lambda-6). Distinct eigenvalues imply diagonalizability, so diag(7,6) is a Jordan canonical form, original option a (index 0). The worked solution explicitly derives this result and saved grading reports Correct. The stored [[7,1],[0,7]] has trace 14 and determinant 49, whereas A has trace 13 and determinant 42, so it cannot be similar to A. This is a derived source-backed correction, not a direct MA answer key."}] "usage" {"q-108719" [] "e-9225" [] "e-9087" [] "q-112453" [] "e-9226" []} "basis" 2505 "retractions" [[17592186176147 :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186176204 :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186176204 :question/answer-fields 17592186173886] [17592186175761 :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186175761 :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J$, we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}5 - λ & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(5 - λ)(-2 - λ) - 4 \\cdot 2 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 3λ - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 6)(λ + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=6\n$$\n\nSince we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note**: A Jordan canonical form is unique up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. Therefore, another Jordan canonical form for this matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186175784 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186175784 :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186175783 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186175783 :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J$, we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(-4 - λ) - 3 \\cdot (-3) &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)(λ + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186173545 17592186173546 17592186173547 17592186173548 17592186173549 17592186173887 17592186173888 17592186173889 17592186173890 17592186173891] "immutable_fields" [[17592186173544 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173544 :answer-field/id] [17592186173544 :answer-field/key] [17592186173544 :answer-field/type] [17592186173886 :answer-field/correct] [175921